items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:37:---
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:38:
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:39:## Definition
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:40:
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:41:Let $\kappa$ and $\lambda$ be cardinals ([[def-cardinal]]), and recall the
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:42:notation of [[lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined]]:
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:43:
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:44:$$\kappa \sqcup \lambda = (\{0\} \times \kappa) \cup (\{1\} \times \lambda), \qquad {}^{\lambda}\kappa = \{\, h : h \text{ is a function } \lambda \to \kappa \,\}.$$
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:45:
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:46:**Sum and product.**
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:47:
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:48:$$\kappa \oplus \lambda \;:=\; \lvert \kappa \sqcup \lambda \rvert, \qquad \kappa \otimes \lambda \;:=\; \lvert \kappa \times \lambda \rvert .$$
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:49:
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:50:Both values exist in ZF and are cardinals: claim (c) of
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:51:[[lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined]] well-orders each of the two sets
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:52:explicitly, and [[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]] then supplies the
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:53:least equinumerous ordinal. **No choice principle is used.**
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:54:
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:55:**Exponentiation.**
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:56:
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:57:$$\kappa^{\lambda} \;:=\; \lvert {}^{\lambda}\kappa \rvert ,$$
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:58:
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:59:the number of functions from a set of size $\lambda$ to a set of size $\kappa$.
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:60:The right-hand side is defined exactly when ${}^{\lambda}\kappa$ is
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:61:well-orderable. **Assuming the Axiom of Choice** ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) every
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:62:set is well-orderable ([[thm-well-ordering-theorem]]) and $\kappa^{\lambda}$ is
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:63:defined for all cardinals; every statement on this page that writes
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:64:$\kappa^{\lambda}$ for an infinite exponent says so in its own hypotheses.
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:65:
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:66:**Transport to arbitrary sets.** If $A$ and $B$ are well-orderable with
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:67:$\lvert A \rvert = \kappa$ and $\lvert B \rvert = \lambda$, then
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:68:
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:69:$$\lvert A \sqcup B \rvert = \kappa \oplus \lambda, \qquad \lvert A \times B \rvert = \kappa \otimes \lambda, \qquad \lvert {}^{B}A \rvert = \kappa^{\lambda}$$
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:70:
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:71:whenever the sets on the left have cardinalities at all, because $A \approx \kappa$
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:72:and $B \approx \lambda$ ([[def-equinumerous]]) and the three constructions
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:73:respect $\approx$ (claim (a) of [[lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined]],
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:74:[[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]). So the operations may be computed from
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:75:any representatives.
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:76:
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:77:**Finite and infinite cardinals.** A cardinal $\kappa$ is **finite** when
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:78:$\kappa \in \omega$ and **infinite** when $\omega \subseteq \kappa$, that is
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:79:$\omega \le \kappa$; by trichotomy ([[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]]) and
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:80:[[lem-omega-least-limit-ordinal]] every cardinal is exactly one of the two.
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:81:
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:82:## Remarks
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:83:
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:84:**The symbols $\oplus$ and $\otimes$ are not decoration.** Ordinal addition and
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:85:ordinal multiplication ([[def-ordinal-addition]], [[def-ordinal-multiplication]])
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:86:are defined on **the same objects** — cardinals are ordinals — and give
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:87:**different values**. With $\omega$ read as a cardinal,
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:88:$\omega \oplus \omega = \omega$, whereas the ordinal sum $\omega + \omega$ is
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:89:strictly larger than $\omega$; and $\omega \otimes \omega = \omega$, whereas the
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:90:ordinal product $\omega \cdot \omega$ is larger still. Writing both operations
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:91:with $+$ and $\cdot$ would make every equation on this page ambiguous, so the
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:92:cardinal operations get their own symbols and the plain $+$ and $\cdot$ on this
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:93:page always mean the ordinal ones.
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:94:
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:95:**Exponentiation keeps the symbol, under a hard rule.** There is no comparably
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:96:readable alternative to $\kappa^{\lambda}$, and
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:97:[[rem-ordinal-versus-cardinal-exponentiation]] already records that
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:98:$\alpha^{\beta}$ is used for two different operations: as ordinals
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:99:$2^{\omega} = \omega$, while the cardinal $2^{\omega}$ counts the functions
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:100:$\omega \to \{0,1\}$ and is uncountable. The rule adopted here, and followed on
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:101:this page and its companion, is:
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:102:
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:103:> **In an exponential, the base and the exponent are always alephs, letters or
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:104:> expressions denoting cardinals — $\kappa$, $\lambda$, $\mu$,
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:105:> $\mathfrak{c}$, $\operatorname{cf}(\kappa)$, $\lvert A \rvert$ — or a natural
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:106:> number read as a cardinal; never $\omega$, never $\omega_1$, and never a
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:107:> letter denoting an ordinal, such as $\alpha, \beta, \gamma, \xi, \eta$.**
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:108:
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:109:So $2^{\aleph_0}$, $\kappa^{\lambda}$ and $\aleph_1^{\aleph_0}$ are cardinal
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:110:exponentials, and an expression such as $\omega^{\omega}$ or $\alpha^{\beta}$ is
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:111:never written here at all. Where a value has to be named in both readings, the
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:112:two are given different letters.
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:113:
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:114:**What is being counted, in each case.** $\kappa \oplus \lambda$ is the size of
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:115:two disjoint blocks laid side by side; the tagging in $\sqcup$ is what makes
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:116:"disjoint" true even though one of $\kappa$ and $\lambda$ is always a subset of
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:117:the other, so their intersection is the smaller of the two. $\kappa \otimes \lambda$
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:118:is the size of a rectangle. $\kappa^{\lambda}$ is the number of ways to choose a
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:119:value in $\kappa$ for each of $\lambda$ positions, independently. None of the
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:120:three is sensitive to the order in which the elements are arranged, which is
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:121:exactly what distinguishes them from the ordinal operations
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:122:([[def-ordinal-exponentiation]] included), whose values depend on the arrangement.
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:123:
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:124:**The zero and one cases are not special.** $0 = \varnothing$ and $1 = \{0\}$ are
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:125:cardinals ([[def-ordinal]]), and the definitions apply to them unchanged:
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:126:$\kappa \sqcup 0 = \{0\} \times \kappa$, $\kappa \times 0 = \varnothing$, and
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:127:${}^{0}\kappa = \{\varnothing\}$ has exactly one element, the empty function.
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:128:The resulting unit laws are proved rather than stipulated, in
items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md:129:[[lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws]].

