items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:31:---
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:32:
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:33:## Definition
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:34:
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:35:Let $\alpha$ be an ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]). A subset $C \subseteq \alpha$ is
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:36:**cofinal in $\alpha$**, equivalently **unbounded in $\alpha$**, when
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:37:
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:38:$$\text{for every } \xi \in \alpha \text{ there is } \eta \in C \text{ with } \xi \le \eta.$$
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:39:
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:40:A subset that is not cofinal is **bounded below $\alpha$**: there is
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:41:$\xi \in \alpha$ such that $\eta < \xi$ for every $\eta \in C$.
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:42:
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:43:## Remarks
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:44:
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:45:- **At a limit ordinal, cofinal means the supremum is attained from below.** If
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:46:  $\lambda$ is a limit ordinal ([[def-limit-ordinal]]) and $C \subseteq \lambda$
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:47:  is nonempty, then $C$ is cofinal in $\lambda$ if and only if
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:48:  $\sup C = \bigcup C = \lambda$ (claim (e) of [[lem-ordinal-basics]]). If
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:49:  $\bigcup C = \lambda$, then every $\xi \in \lambda$ lies in some $\eta \in C$,
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:50:  so $\xi < \eta$ and $C$ is cofinal. Conversely, if $C$ is cofinal then
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:51:  $\bigcup C \subseteq \lambda$, because each $\eta \in C$ satisfies
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:52:  $\eta \subseteq \lambda$ by transitivity; and for $\xi \in \lambda$ the ordinal
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:53:  $\xi^{+}$ again lies in $\lambda$ ([[def-limit-ordinal]]), so cofinality supplies
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:54:  $\eta \in C$ with $\xi^{+} \le \eta$, whence $\xi \in \xi^{+} \subseteq \eta$ and
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:55:  $\xi \in \bigcup C$, giving $\lambda \subseteq \bigcup C$. This is the form in which the
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:56:  notion is used on this page, and it is exactly the hypothesis of the
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:57:  continuity clause of [[thm-ordinal-arithmetic-monotonicity]].
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:58:
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:59:- **At $0$ and at successors the notion is degenerate.** $\varnothing$ is
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:60:  cofinal in $0$, vacuously, and it is the only subset of $0$. If
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:61:  $\alpha = \delta^{+}$ then $\delta$ is the greatest element of $\alpha$
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:62:  ([[lem-ordinal-basics]], [[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]]), so a subset is cofinal
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:63:  in $\alpha$ if and only if it contains $\delta$. The interesting case is the
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:64:  limit case, and that is where the notion is used.
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:65:
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:66:- **What is not defined at this point in the reading order.** The **cofinality**
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:67:  $\operatorname{cf}(\alpha)$, the least order type of a cofinal subset, and the
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:68:  vocabulary of **regular** and **singular** cardinals, are not introduced here;
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:69:  they are introduced later, on Cardinal Arithmetic, Cofinality and the Alephs.
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:70:  Nothing on this page needs them: the boundedness theorem below is stated as
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:71:  "no at most countable subset is cofinal", which is a statement about subsets
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:72:  and not about a cardinal invariant.
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:73:
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:74:- **Cofinal is a property of the pair, not of the set.** $\omega$ is cofinal in
items/def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md:75:  $\omega$ and bounded below $\omega + 1$. The ordinal must always be named.
