items/def-ordinal.md:40:---
items/def-ordinal.md:41:
items/def-ordinal.md:42:## Definition
items/def-ordinal.md:43:
items/def-ordinal.md:44:A set $\alpha$ is an **ordinal** when both of the following hold.
items/def-ordinal.md:45:
items/def-ordinal.md:46:1. $\alpha$ is a **transitive set**: every element of $\alpha$ is also a subset
items/def-ordinal.md:47:   of $\alpha$, that is $x \in \alpha \Rightarrow x \subseteq \alpha$.
items/def-ordinal.md:48:2. The membership relation restricted to $\alpha$, namely
items/def-ordinal.md:49:   $\{(x, y) \in \alpha \times \alpha : x \in y\}$, is a **strict well-order**
items/def-ordinal.md:50:   of $\alpha$ ([[def-well-order]]): it is irreflexive, transitive as a
items/def-ordinal.md:51:   relation, trichotomous on $\alpha$, and every nonempty subset of $\alpha$ has
items/def-ordinal.md:52:   an $\in$-least element.
items/def-ordinal.md:53:
items/def-ordinal.md:54:Ordinals are written with lowercase Greek letters, and for ordinals we set
items/def-ordinal.md:55:
items/def-ordinal.md:56:$$\alpha < \beta :\iff \alpha \in \beta, \qquad \alpha \le \beta :\iff (\alpha \in \beta \text{ or } \alpha = \beta).$$
items/def-ordinal.md:57:
items/def-ordinal.md:58:Write $0 := \emptyset$, which is an ordinal because both clauses hold vacuously,
items/def-ordinal.md:59:and write $\alpha^{+} := \alpha \cup \{\alpha\}$ for the **successor** of
items/def-ordinal.md:60:$\alpha$.
items/def-ordinal.md:61:
items/def-ordinal.md:62:## Remarks
items/def-ordinal.md:63:
items/def-ordinal.md:64:- **Two different transitivities.** Clause 1 is about the *set* $\alpha$: it
items/def-ordinal.md:65:  contains all members of its members. Clause 2 asks in part that the *relation*
items/def-ordinal.md:66:  $\in$ be transitive on $\alpha$. If every element of $\alpha$ is itself a
items/def-ordinal.md:67:  transitive set then the relation $\in$ is transitive on $\alpha$; the converse
items/def-ordinal.md:68:  holds *given clause 1*, and fails without it. For
items/def-ordinal.md:69:  $\alpha = \{\{\{\emptyset\}\}\}$ the relation
items/def-ordinal.md:70:  $\{(x, y) \in \alpha \times \alpha : x \in y\}$ is empty, hence vacuously
items/def-ordinal.md:71:  transitive, yet the sole element $\{\{\emptyset\}\}$ of $\alpha$ is not a
items/def-ordinal.md:72:  transitive set, since $\{\emptyset\} \in \{\{\emptyset\}\}$ while
items/def-ordinal.md:73:  $\emptyset \notin \{\{\emptyset\}\}$. Under clause 1 the two readings do
items/def-ordinal.md:74:  coincide, because then every member of a member of $\alpha$ again lies in
items/def-ordinal.md:75:  $\alpha$, where the relation has something to say. Neither clause implies the
items/def-ordinal.md:76:  other, and both are needed.
items/def-ordinal.md:77:- **Every ordinal is literally the set of all smaller ordinals.** By clause 1 and
items/def-ordinal.md:78:  the notation above, $\alpha = \{\beta : \beta < \alpha\}$, so the ordinals
items/def-ordinal.md:79:  carry their own order relation as membership. This is von Neumann's device,
items/def-ordinal.md:80:  and it is what makes it unnecessary to define an order type as an equivalence
items/def-ordinal.md:81:  class of well-orders: apart from the class of the empty well-order, which is the
items/def-ordinal.md:82:  singleton $\{\varnothing\}$, such a class is a proper class rather than a set,
items/def-ordinal.md:83:  whereas the representative chosen here is always a set.
items/def-ordinal.md:84:- **The natural numbers are ordinals, and so is $\omega$.** Each natural number
items/def-ordinal.md:85:  is a transitive set ([[lem-nat-transitive-irreflexive]]), and on $\mathbb{N}$
items/def-ordinal.md:86:  membership coincides with the usual strict order, so each natural number and
items/def-ordinal.md:87:  $\mathbb{N} = \omega$ itself ([[def-natural-numbers]]) satisfy both clauses.
items/def-ordinal.md:88:  Both facts are proved in [[lem-omega-least-limit-ordinal]], where they are
items/def-ordinal.md:89:  needed; they are recorded here because they are the intended picture.
items/def-ordinal.md:90:- **The Axiom of Foundation is not used.** Some treatments define an ordinal as a
items/def-ordinal.md:91:  transitive set *linearly* ordered by $\in$, which is equivalent to the
items/def-ordinal.md:92:  definition above only in the presence of Foundation, since Foundation is what
items/def-ordinal.md:93:  supplies the least element. This library does not assume Foundation anywhere,
items/def-ordinal.md:94:  so the least element property is written into the definition instead.
items/def-ordinal.md:95:- The definition is absolute in a strong sense: whether a set is an ordinal
items/def-ordinal.md:96:  depends only on its members and the membership relation among them, with no
items/def-ordinal.md:97:  reference to any ambient construction. That is why ordinals can be used to
items/def-ordinal.md:98:  index constructions in any model of ZF without further hypotheses.

