items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:35:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:36:## Statement
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:37:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:38:Let $\operatorname{cf}$, regular and singular be as in [[def-cofinality]], and
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:39:let $\aleph_\alpha$ be as in [[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]]. Then:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:40:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:41:**(a) In ZF.** $\operatorname{cf}(\aleph_0) = \aleph_0$, so $\aleph_0$ is
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:42:regular.
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:43:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:44:**(b) Assuming the Axiom of Choice** ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]).
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:45:$\aleph_{\alpha+1}$ is regular for every ordinal $\alpha$.
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:46:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:47:**(c) In ZF.** $\operatorname{cf}(\aleph_\omega) = \aleph_0$, and
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:48:$\aleph_0 < \aleph_\omega$, so $\aleph_\omega$ is **singular**.
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:49:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:50:**(d) Assuming the Axiom of Choice.** Every infinite cardinal below
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:51:$\aleph_\omega$ is regular, so $\aleph_\omega$ is the least singular infinite
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:52:cardinal.
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:53:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:54:**Clause (b) is where the Axiom of Choice becomes indispensable, and the
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:55:hypothesis is not decoration.** The proof spends it once, to select an injection
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:56:$g(\xi) \to \aleph_\alpha$ for each $\xi$ below the cofinality, and there is no
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:57:canonical such family to fall back on: the sets $g(\xi)$ are ordinals, but the
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:58:injections are not determined by them. Clauses (a), (c) and the classification
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:59:half of (d) are choice free.
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:60:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:61:## Facts & Assumptions
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:62:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:63:**Given:** ZF, with the Axiom of Choice assumed only in clauses (b) and (d). Throughout, a map is called cofinal when its range is a cofinal subset of the target ([[def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal]]).
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:64:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:65:[L1] $\operatorname{cf}(\alpha) \le \alpha$; for a limit ordinal $\lambda$, $\operatorname{cf}(\lambda)$ is an infinite cardinal; every cofinal $C \subseteq \lambda$ has $\operatorname{cf}(\lambda) \le \lvert C\rvert$ ([[thm-cofinality-basics]], [[def-cofinality]]).
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:66:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:67:[L2] $\operatorname{cf}(\alpha)$ is the least length of a cofinal map into $\alpha$, and a strictly increasing cofinal map of that length exists ([[def-cofinality]], [[lem-cofinality-is-well-defined]]).
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:68:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:69:[L3] $\aleph_0 = \omega$; $\aleph_{\alpha+1} = \aleph_\alpha^{+}$ is the least cardinal strictly above $\aleph_\alpha$; $\aleph_\lambda = \bigcup\{\aleph_\beta : \beta \in \lambda\}$ at limits; every $\aleph_\alpha$ is an infinite cardinal and the operation is strictly increasing ([[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]], [[cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined]], [[lem-successor-cardinal-exists]]).
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:70:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:71:[L4] Every infinite cardinal is a limit ordinal (claim (b) of [[thm-cardinal-arithmetic-agrees-with-finite-counting]], [[def-limit-ordinal]]), and a cardinal $\kappa$ is infinite exactly when $\omega \subseteq \kappa$, that is $\omega \le \kappa$ ([[def-cardinal-arithmetic]], [[def-cardinal]]).
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:72:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:73:[L5] For an infinite cardinal $\mu$ and a cardinal $\nu$ with $0 \ne \nu \le \mu$, $\mu \otimes \nu = \mu$ ([[cor-cardinal-absorption]], [[thm-hessenberg]], [[def-cardinal-arithmetic]]).
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:74:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:75:[L6] For cardinals $\kappa \le \lambda$ iff $\kappa \preceq \lambda$; $A \preceq B$ with both well-orderable gives $\lvert A\rvert \le \lvert B\rvert$; $\otimes$ is monotone ([[lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws]]).
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:76:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:77:[L7] For a well-orderable set $X$, $\lvert X\rvert$ is the least ordinal equinumerous with $X$, satisfies $X \approx \lvert X\rvert$ and $\lvert \alpha\rvert \le \alpha$, and equals $\alpha$ exactly when $\alpha$ is a cardinal ([[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]], [[def-equinumerous]]).
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:78:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:79:[L8] Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function ([[def-axiom-of-choice]], [[def-choice-function]]).
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:80:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:81:[L9] Ordinals satisfy trichotomy, $\alpha \subseteq \beta$ iff $\alpha \in \beta$ or $\alpha = \beta$, the union of a set of ordinals is its least upper bound, every nonempty set of ordinals has an $\in$-least element, $\omega$ is the least limit ordinal, and every strictly increasing map of ordinals is injective ([[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]], [[lem-ordinal-basics]], [[lem-omega-least-limit-ordinal]], [[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]).
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:82:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:83:[L10] Every infinite cardinal is $\aleph_\alpha$ for exactly one ordinal $\alpha$ ([[thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph]]).
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:84:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:85:## Proof
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:86:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:87:**Proof technique:** direct.
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:88:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:89:1.1 Claim (a): $\omega$ is a limit ordinal by [L9], so $\operatorname{cf}(\omega)$ is an infinite cardinal by [L1], hence $\omega \le \operatorname{cf}(\omega)$ by [L4]; and $\operatorname{cf}(\omega) \le \omega$ by [L1], so $\operatorname{cf}(\aleph_0) = \aleph_0$. [L1, L3, L4, L9]
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:90:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:91:1.2 The set $C = \{\aleph_n : n \in \omega\}$ exists by Replacement, is contained in $\aleph_\omega$ and is cofinal in it, since $\aleph_\omega = \bigcup C$ by [L3] means every $\zeta \in \aleph_\omega$ lies in some $\aleph_n$ and hence satisfies $\zeta \le \aleph_n$; moreover $n \mapsto \aleph_n$ is injective by the strict increase in [L3], so $C \approx \omega$ and $\lvert C\rvert = \aleph_0$ by [L7]. [L3, L7, L9]
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:92:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:93:1.3 Setting up claim (b): let $\lambda = \aleph_\alpha$, $\kappa = \aleph_{\alpha+1}$, and suppose $\beta = \operatorname{cf}(\kappa) < \kappa$, with $g : \beta \to \kappa$ strictly increasing and cofinal by [L2]; then $\kappa$ is a limit ordinal by [L4], so $\beta$ is an infinite cardinal by [L1], and $\beta \le \lambda$ because $\beta$ is a cardinal below the least cardinal strictly above $\lambda$ ([L3]); moreover $\kappa = \bigcup\{g(\xi) : \xi \in \beta\}$, since for $\zeta \in \kappa$ the ordinal $\zeta \cup \{\zeta\}$ also lies in $\kappa$ and is $\le g(\xi)$ for some $\xi$, putting $\zeta \in g(\xi)$. [L1, L2, L3, L4, L9]
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:94:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:95:2.1 Claim (c): step 1.2 and [L1] give $\operatorname{cf}(\aleph_\omega) \le \aleph_0$; and $\aleph_\omega$ is an infinite cardinal, hence a limit ordinal by [L4], so $\operatorname{cf}(\aleph_\omega)$ is an infinite cardinal and $\aleph_0 \le \operatorname{cf}(\aleph_\omega)$ by [L1] and [L4]; therefore $\operatorname{cf}(\aleph_\omega) = \aleph_0 < \aleph_\omega$ by the strict increase in [L3], and $\aleph_\omega$ is singular. [step 1.2, L1, L3, L4]
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:96:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:97:2.2 Claim (b): each $g(\xi)$ of step 1.3 lies in $\kappa$, so $\lvert g(\xi)\rvert$ is a cardinal below $\kappa$ and hence $\lvert g(\xi)\rvert \le \lambda$ by [L3] and [L7], and the set $I_\xi$ of injections $g(\xi) \to \lambda$ is nonempty; a choice function from [L8] on $\{I_\xi : \xi \in \beta\}$ supplies injections $e_\xi : g(\xi) \to \lambda$ for all $\xi$ at once, and $\zeta \mapsto (\xi_\zeta, e_{\xi_\zeta}(\zeta))$, with $\xi_\zeta$ the $\in$-least $\xi$ having $\zeta \in g(\xi)$, is then an injection $\kappa \to \beta \times \lambda$; so $\kappa \le \beta \otimes \lambda = \lambda$ by [L6] and [L5], contradicting $\lambda < \kappa$, and therefore $\operatorname{cf}(\kappa) = \kappa$. [step 1.3, L5, L6, L7, L8, L9]
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:98:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:99:3.1 Claim (d) and the conclusion: an infinite cardinal $\kappa < \aleph_\omega$ is $\aleph_\alpha$ for exactly one $\alpha$ by [L10], and $\alpha \in \omega$, since $\omega \le \alpha$ would give $\aleph_\omega \le \aleph_\alpha$ by the strict increase in [L3]; so $\kappa$ is $\aleph_0$, regular by step 1.1, or $\aleph_{n+1}$ for some $n \in \omega$, regular by step 2.2; with step 2.1 this makes $\aleph_\omega$ the least singular infinite cardinal. [step 1.1, step 2.1, step 2.2, L3, L9, L10] ∎
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:100:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:101:## Remarks
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:102:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:103:**Why regularity of $\aleph_1$ is not a theorem of ZF.** The proof of clause (b) selects one injection for each $\xi$ below the cofinality, and that selection is the entire content of the choice hypothesis: a union of countably many countable sets is not provably countable in ZF, and the same phenomenon is what would otherwise force $\operatorname{cf}(\aleph_1) = \aleph_1$. The choice ledger at the end of this page records how far this can fail.
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:104:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:105:**Why $\aleph_\omega$ is singular for a completely different reason.** Nothing is chosen in clause (c): the map $n \mapsto \aleph_n$ is definable, and it is short and cofinal simply because the index $\omega$ is a limit ordinal reached from below in $\omega$ steps. Singularity of $\aleph_\omega$ is therefore a fact about the **index**, not about the size, and clause (c) holds in ZF.
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:106:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:107:**What "least singular" means and what it does not.** Clause (d) locates $\aleph_\omega$ among the alephs: everything below it is regular, under choice. It says nothing about which cardinals above $\aleph_\omega$ are singular, and it says nothing about $2^{\aleph_0}$, whose position in the aleph hierarchy is not determined by anything on this page. What **is** determined is a constraint on that position, and it is [[cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power]].

