     1	---
     2	page: cardinal-arithmetic-and-cofinality
     3	title: "Cardinal Arithmetic, Cofinality and the Alephs"
     4	status: published
     5	items: [lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set,
     6	        lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined,
     7	        def-cardinal-arithmetic,
     8	        lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws,
     9	        thm-cardinal-arithmetic-agrees-with-finite-counting,
    10	        thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor,
    11	        thm-hessenberg,
    12	        cor-cardinal-absorption,
    13	        lem-successor-cardinal-exists,
    14	        cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined,
    15	        def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies,
    16	        thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph,
    17	        thm-cardinal-comparability-iff-ac,
    18	        thm-tarski-square,
    19	        lem-cofinality-is-well-defined,
    20	        def-cofinality,
    21	        thm-cofinality-basics,
    22	        thm-regularity-of-the-alephs,
    23	        def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product,
    24	        thm-konig,
    25	        cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power,
    26	        rem-cardinal-arithmetic-choice-ledger]
    27	examples: [fs-cardinal-addition-is-cancellative,
    28	           fs-every-aleph-is-regular,
    29	           fs-cardinal-exponentiation-is-strictly-monotone-in-the-base,
    30	           fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega]
    31	---
    32	
    33	**Objective.** A cardinal is an ordinal not equinumerous with any smaller ordinal
    34	([[def-cardinal]]), and that much is already in place. This page turns the
    35	cardinals into an arithmetic: it defines $\kappa \oplus \lambda$,
    36	$\kappa \otimes \lambda$ and $\kappa^{\lambda}$, proves the laws they satisfy and
    37	the laws they do not, builds the aleph and beth hierarchies, shows that the
    38	alephs exhaust the infinite cardinals, defines the cofinality
    39	$\operatorname{cf}(\alpha)$ with the regular and singular vocabulary, and proves
    40	König's inequality and the constraint it puts on $2^{\aleph_0}$. Throughout, each
    41	result carries its own choice hypothesis, and
    42	[[rem-cardinal-arithmetic-choice-ledger]] keeps the ledger.
    43	
    44	**A choice-free notion of cardinality has to come first.** [[def-cardinal]]
    45	attaches $\lvert X\rvert$ to a set under the hypothesis "Assume the Axiom of
    46	Choice", and it needs that hypothesis only to know that $X$ carries a well-order
    47	at all. [[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]] isolates the rest: for a
    48	**well-orderable** $X$ there is a least ordinal equinumerous with it, that ordinal
    49	is a cardinal, and equinumerous sets receive the same one, all in ZF. Without
    50	this item Hessenberg's theorem could not be stated as the theorem of ZF that it
    51	is, and Tarski's theorem — which is precisely about the gap between ZF and ZFC —
    52	could not be stated at all.
    53	
    54	**Sum and product are free; exponentiation is not.** The disjoint union and the cartesian
    55	product of two ordinals carry well-orders written down from the ordinal order
    56	([[lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined]]), so $\oplus$ and $\otimes$ are ZF
    57	operations. A set of *functions* between well-ordered sets has no canonical
    58	order, so $\kappa^{\lambda}$ is defined under the Axiom of Choice, and every
    59	statement here that writes an infinite exponential says so in its own hypotheses.
    60	
    61	**The symbols are deliberately not $+$ and $\cdot$.** Ordinal addition and
    62	multiplication are defined on the same objects and give different values, so the
    63	cardinal operations are written $\oplus$ and $\otimes$
    64	([[def-cardinal-arithmetic]]). Exponentiation keeps its notation under an
    65	explicit rule stated where it is defined: base and exponent are always alephs or
    66	one of $\kappa, \lambda, \mu$, never $\omega$ and never an ordinal letter. That
    67	rule is the answer to the warning already recorded in
    68	[[rem-ordinal-versus-cardinal-exponentiation]]. A second collision is handled the
    69	same way: $\kappa^{+}$ on a cardinal letter is the successor cardinal, while
    70	$\alpha^{+}$ on an ordinal letter keeps its published meaning, and the two are
    71	never the same on an infinite cardinal.
    72	
    73	**One notation, one meaning, on the finite numbers.**
    74	[[def-finite-cardinality]] already writes $\lvert A\rvert$ for a finite set, with
    75	a natural number as its value. [[thm-cardinal-arithmetic-agrees-with-finite-counting]]
    76	proves that this is the same $\lvert A\rvert$, and that on $\omega$ the cardinal
    77	operations are the published counting operations. It also settles the small facts
    78	the rest of the page leans on constantly: every natural number and $\omega$ are
    79	cardinals, and every infinite cardinal is a limit ordinal.
    80	
    81	**Hessenberg's theorem is the engine.** $\kappa \otimes \kappa = \kappa$ for
    82	every infinite cardinal, proved from a single well-order of $\kappa \times \kappa$
    83	that orders pairs by their maximum first ([[thm-hessenberg]]). Nothing is chosen:
    84	the order is defined, not selected, so the theorem is ZF. Everything about
    85	$\oplus$ and $\otimes$ on infinite cardinals collapses out of it —
    86	[[cor-cardinal-absorption]] says the larger argument simply swallows the smaller
    87	— and the collapse is what makes cancellation fail, which section 5 records.
    88	
    89	**The hierarchy, and its exhaustiveness.** The Hartogs number gives a successor
    90	cardinal in ZF ([[lem-successor-cardinal-exists]]); transfinite recursion along
    91	the ordinals turns the successor clause into the alephs, and the power clause
    92	into the beths ([[cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined]],
    93	[[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]]). That the alephs are not merely a supply of
    94	infinite cardinals but **all** of them is a separate theorem, proved by a least
    95	counterexample rather than by the recursion, and its second clause — that every
    96	infinite *set* is equinumerous with an aleph — is where the Axiom of Choice
    97	enters ([[thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph]]).
    98	
    99	**Where choice is measured exactly.** Comparability of arbitrary sets is
   100	equivalent to the Axiom of Choice ([[thm-cardinal-comparability-iff-ac]]), so the
   101	trichotomy this page uses freely for cardinals is *not* available for sets. And
   102	Tarski's square law, $A \times A \approx A$ for every infinite set, is also
   103	equivalent to it ([[thm-tarski-square]]): extending Hessenberg's theorem from the
   104	alephs to arbitrary sets is not a mild strengthening but choice itself.
   105	
   106	**Cofinality.** [[def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal]] supplies cofinal subsets and
   107	stops there. Here the cofinality function is defined:
   108	$\operatorname{cf}(\alpha)$ is the least length of a family reaching $\alpha$ from
   109	below ([[lem-cofinality-is-well-defined]], [[def-cofinality]]), and for a limit
   110	ordinal it is an infinite cardinal which is its own cofinality
   111	([[thm-cofinality-basics]]). Regularity of $\aleph_0$ and singularity of
   112	$\aleph_\omega$ are theorems of ZF; regularity of the successor alephs is proved
   113	here only from the Axiom of Choice, and [[thm-regularity-of-the-alephs]] carries
   114	the hypothesis in its statement and names where it is spent.
   115	
   116	**König, and what ZFC decides about the continuum.** With sums and
   117	products of indexed families in place ([[def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product]]),
   118	[[thm-konig]] gives $\sum_i \kappa_i < \prod_i \lambda_i$ whenever
   119	$\kappa_i < \lambda_i$ throughout — Cantor's diagonal argument over an arbitrary
   120	index set. Its consequence is $\kappa < \kappa^{\operatorname{cf}(\kappa)}$ and
   121	hence $\operatorname{cf}(2^{\kappa}) > \kappa$
   122	([[cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power]]). At $\kappa = \aleph_0$ that says the
   123	continuum has uncountable cofinality, which excludes candidate values without
   124	selecting one.
   125	

